stake concerning the authorship of a work has been caused by a manuscript. I do not mean the deliberate plagiarisms committed even by a giant like Handel, e.g. in \hich\f0 \lquote \loch\f0 Israel in Egypt\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
. I think rather of cases like the \hich\f0 \lquote \loch\f0 St. Luke Passion\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0  by an unknown author,\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0  \hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
which has long been attributed to J. S. Bach only because of a manuscript in his hand\-writing, or of a piece from Gluck\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s \hich\f0 \lquote \loch\f0 Alceste\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 \hich\f0  which found its way into K\'f6
\loch\f0 chel\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s Mozart catalogue as an Andantino for piano, in consequence of a manuscript in Mozart\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 s wr\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 i\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
ting. A well-known and beautiful organ concerto in D minor would still be looked on today as the most important work of Wilhelm Friedemann Bach, were it not that the original of this composition had been found in a printed Concerto Grosso by Vivaldi. It i
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 s\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 
 irrelevant to question why Bach or Mozart took the trouble of copying music, for at that time handwriting was still the usual way of reproduction and performing music was a much more private affair then than now. Bach often copied music for his own use, 
\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 a\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 nd so did every working musician of that time. It is equally irrelevant to ask why Michael Haydn copied a symphony or half a symphony of his brother\hich\f0 \rquote \loch\f0 
s. The manuscript that misled Perger proves only by whose hand it was written, not in whose head it was co\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 m\hich\af0\dbch\af13\loch\f0 posed. All the further}{
